.Dd November 1, 2026
.Dt SW_VERS 1
.Os NextBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm sw_vers
.Nd print the operating system version
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Nm
.Fl productName
.Nm
.Fl productVersion
.Nm
.Fl buildVersion
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
prints the name, version and build of the installed operating system.
With no option it prints all three, one per line, each after a label and a
tab:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ProductName:	NextBSD
ProductVersion:	27.0.0
BuildVersion:	42
.Ed
.Pp
The options print one value alone:
.Bl -tag -width "-productVersion"
.It Fl productName
The product name,
.Ql NextBSD .
.It Fl productVersion
The product version, which matches the kernel's
.Ql uname -r .
.It Fl buildVersion
The build number of the installed
.Li NextBSD-everything
package.
.El
.Pp
The installed packages are versioned
.Ar version . Ns Ar build ,
so
.Ql pkg info NextBSD-everything
shows the same two values.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Pa /System/Library/CoreServices/SystemVersion.plist
Where the values come from:
.Li ProductName ,
.Li ProductVersion
and
.Li ProductBuildVersion .
The
.Li NextBSD-everything
package installs it, and
.Ql pkg upgrade
replaces it.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Nm
fails, with a message on standard error, when the property list is missing
(a system installed before it was introduced), cannot be read, or lacks one of
the keys.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr nextbsd-fetch 1 ,
.Xr uname 1 ,
.Xr pkg 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
first appeared in Mac OS X.
The NextBSD version is ported from Darwin's DarwinTools.
